---
import Page from '../../layouts/Page.astro';
import { breadcrumbs } from '../../components/_breadcrumbs';
import { downloads } from '../../config/downloads';

const crumbs = breadcrumbs([{ name: 'Compare', href: '/compare/' }, { name: 'Niyora vs Moody Month', href: '/compare/niyora-vs-moody-month/' }]);
---

<Page
  title="Niyora vs Moody Month: trends vs the moment itself · Niyora"
  description="Moody Month tracks your mood and shows you trends, then stops. Niyora predicts your PMS window too, then helps you through the days it points to."
  canonicalUrl="https://niyora.com/compare/niyora-vs-moody-month/"
  jsonLd={crumbs}
>
  <section class="s hero">
    <div class="eyebrow">Niyora vs Moody Month</div>
    <h1>Moody Month tracks your mood. Niyora helps you through the hard hour.</h1>
    <p class="lead">Moody Month is a mood and cycle tracker. You log how you feel, it maps that against your cycle, it shows you trends, and there it stops. Niyora tracks and predicts your PMS window too, but it does not stop at the trend line. When the feeling actually lands, it helps you reflect, regulate, and respond in about a minute, and its training builds the skill before you need it.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="s">
    <div class="moat">
      <p>A mood tracker tells you it is coming, then leaves you to handle it. Niyora predicts the same window, then <strong>shows up when it lands</strong>: reflect, regulate, respond in about a minute, and training that builds the skill before you need it. A trend line is where a tracker finishes. For Niyora it is where the help starts.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="s">
    <h2>The difference, line by line</h2>
    <div class="compare-grid">
      <div class="compare-head them">A mood tracker like Moody Month</div>
      <div class="compare-head us">Niyora</div>

      <div class="compare-cell them"><p>Maps your mood against your cycle and charts the trend. Once it charts, its job is done.</p></div>
      <div class="compare-cell us"><p>Tracks and predicts your window too, then walks you through reflect, regulate, respond when it lands.</p></div>

      <div class="compare-cell them"><p>You feed it data, and the trend line is the finish line.</p></div>
      <div class="compare-cell us"><p>Training builds the skill before the window, so it lands lighter when it comes.</p></div>

      <div class="compare-cell them"><p>Emotional support is a chart of how your mood moved over time.</p></div>
      <div class="compare-cell us"><p>A way to see the moment differently, settle your body, and respond without regret.</p></div>

      <div class="compare-cell them"><p>Success is a more complete record of your moods over time.</p></div>
      <div class="compare-cell us"><p>Success is you moving through the moment, and needing it less.</p></div>

      <div class="compare-cell them"><p>Tracks mood across the whole cycle, every day of the month.</p></div>
      <div class="compare-cell us"><p>Built for one window only. The days before your period, when feelings run high.</p></div>

      <div class="compare-cell them"><p>Your mood history lives in an account so it can chart trends over time.</p></div>
      <div class="compare-cell us"><p>No account, no sign in. Your personal details are stripped before anything reaches the AI.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="s">
    <div class="honest">
      <h2>To be fair to Moody Month</h2>
      <p>Seeing your mood mapped against your cycle can be a real insight, and for some people that pattern is worth having. Niyora tracks and predicts too, so this is not tracker versus no tracker. The difference is what happens after the trend line: Moody Month shows you the pattern, Niyora helps you through the days it points to. Many people will want both.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="s">
    <h2>Not sure the hard week is even PMS?</h2>
    <p class="lead">Take the two-minute check and see how your premenstrual week actually hits.</p>
    <div class="cta-row">
      <a href="/quiz" class="btn">Take the quiz</a>
      <a href={downloads.appStore} class="btn ghost">Get Niyora</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Page>

<style>
  /* Page-specific only: the moat callout, the line-by-line grid, and the
     to-be-fair block. Shared chrome (sheet, sections, dividers, type scale,
     buttons, footer) comes from Page's global CSS. */
  .moat {
    max-width: 720px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 32px 30px;
    border-radius: 20px;
    border: 1px solid rgba(var(--accent-rgb), 0.28);
    background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.03);
  }
  .moat p { font-size: 21px; font-weight: 300; line-height: 1.5; color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.86); max-width: none; margin: 0; }
  .moat strong { color: rgba(var(--accent-rgb), 0.98); font-weight: 500; }

  .compare-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 14px;
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 900px;
    margin: 24px auto 0;
    text-align: left;
  }
  .compare-head { font-size: 12px; letter-spacing: 3px; text-transform: uppercase; padding-bottom: 4px; }
  .compare-head.them { color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.4); }
  .compare-head.us { color: rgba(var(--accent-rgb), 0.9); }
  .compare-cell {
    padding: 18px 20px;
    border-radius: 14px;
    border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.08);
    background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.03);
  }
  .compare-cell p { font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.55; margin: 0; max-width: none; }
  .compare-cell.them { background: rgba(20, 18, 26, 0.4); }
  .compare-cell.them p { color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.52); }
  .compare-cell.us { border-color: rgba(var(--accent-rgb), 0.32); }
  .compare-cell.us p { color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.86); }

  .honest { max-width: 680px; margin: 0 auto; }
  .honest p { color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.72); font-size: 18px; line-height: 1.6; max-width: none; }

  .cta-row { display: flex; gap: 16px; flex-wrap: wrap; justify-content: center; margin-top: 28px; }

  @media (max-width: 720px) {
    .s { padding: 56px 24px; }
    h1 { font-size: 34px; }
    h2 { font-size: 26px; }
    .moat p { font-size: 18px; }
  }
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .compare-grid { gap: 10px 12px; }
    .compare-cell { padding: 14px 16px; }
  }
</style>
